      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Understanding Address"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Pointer is a powerful feature of C++ programming that allows us to work with memory addresses."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "But before we learn about pointers, let's first learn about addresses."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Suppose we have created a variable like this:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "content": "int var;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, a space will be allocated in the computer memory for the var variable, and we can access the memory address using &var."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Let's see an example."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "content": "1#include <iostream>\n2using namespace std;\n3\n4int main() {\n5\n6    int var = 13;\n7\n8    // print the value stored in the variable\n9    cout << \"Value of var: \" << var << endl;\n10\n11\n12    // print the memory address of the variable\n13    cout << \"Address of var: \" << &var;\n14\n15\n16    return 0;\n17}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "Value of var: 13\nAddress of var: 0x7ffc5ff6f594"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, you can see that printing &var gives 0x7ffc5ff6f594, which is the memory location where 13 is stored."
    },
